1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,223
Note Atlas: a local-first research workspace, built on retrieval-augmented generation.

2
00:00:07,017 --> 00:00:11,283
Commercial language models process every prompt on remote servers.

3
00:00:11,283 --> 00:00:16,519
Research notes and unpublished papers leave the device, where they may be logged,

4
00:00:16,519 --> 00:00:17,811
retained, or leaked.

5
00:00:18,617 --> 00:00:22,360
Retrieval-augmented generation changes how a model answers.

6
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:25,912
Instead of relying on what it memorised during training,

7
00:00:25,912 --> 00:00:30,416
the model is handed the most relevant passages from your own documents,

8
00:00:30,416 --> 00:00:33,081
and must answer from them, with citations.

9
00:00:33,883 --> 00:00:37,499
Note Atlas keeps the entire pipeline on one machine.

10
00:00:37,499 --> 00:00:42,575
Backend, vector stores, and the Ollama model server all run on localhost,

11
00:00:42,575 --> 00:00:46,747
across three modules: Boards, Files, and the Research Track.

12
00:00:47,550 --> 00:00:50,510
On a Board, each note is a card on a canvas.

13
00:00:50,510 --> 00:00:55,823
A local model extracts its topics, embeddings suggest related notes as strings,

14
00:00:55,823 --> 00:00:59,859
and a lasso-scoped Ask answers only from the selected notes.

15
00:01:00,650 --> 00:01:06,631
Board Ask retrieves note chunks by semantic similarity, and boosts exact keyword matches.

16
00:01:06,631 --> 00:01:10,596
When nothing scores above the threshold, it does not guess.

17
00:01:10,596 --> 00:01:11,402
It declines.

18
00:01:12,217 --> 00:01:14,455
Files is a classic RAG pipeline.

19
00:01:14,455 --> 00:01:20,122
Documents are split into five-hundred-character chunks, embedded into ChromaDB,

20
00:01:20,122 --> 00:01:24,739
and the three nearest chunks ground a Phi-3 answer, cited by page.

21
00:01:25,550 --> 00:01:29,870
Each enhancement can be switched on independently: Word documents,

22
00:01:29,870 --> 00:01:35,302
keyword search fused with vector search, conversational memory, and a model picker.

23
00:01:35,302 --> 00:01:39,033
The same pipeline also powers a Streamlit laboratory app.

24
00:01:39,817 --> 00:01:41,931
The Research Track goes further.

25
00:01:41,931 --> 00:01:45,961
Every uploaded paper enters a resumable, four-step job queue.

26
00:01:46,750 --> 00:01:47,932
Step one: parse.

27
00:01:47,932 --> 00:01:53,839
A local GROBID service converts the PDF into structured XML: metadata, sections,

28
00:01:53,839 --> 00:01:57,310
and every reference, which is stored as a lead.

29
00:01:58,117 --> 00:01:59,320
Step two: extract.

30
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:04,266
The local model records the research question, method, key results, scope,

31
00:02:04,266 --> 00:02:07,141
and each contribution, as a separate claim.

32
00:02:07,950 --> 00:02:09,557
Step three: limitations.

33
00:02:09,557 --> 00:02:12,502
Each limitation must carry a verbatim quote.

34
00:02:12,502 --> 00:02:15,849
It is kept only if that quote exists in the paper,

35
00:02:15,849 --> 00:02:19,129
and a second check confirms it admits a weakness.

36
00:02:19,917 --> 00:02:21,133
Step four: lineage.

37
00:02:21,133 --> 00:02:24,077
References are ranked by where they are cited,

38
00:02:24,077 --> 00:02:27,597
and the model scores how much the paper builds on each.

39
00:02:27,597 --> 00:02:32,013
The strongest become parent papers, revealing siblings and ancestors.

40
00:02:32,817 --> 00:02:36,262
Completed extractions are indexed for Research Ask.

41
00:02:36,262 --> 00:02:39,707
It searches fields and claims, never the full text,

42
00:02:39,707 --> 00:02:43,355
and every citation names the exact claim it came from.

43
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:48,220
Optional scholarly lookups enrich the library with metadata.

44
00:02:48,220 --> 00:02:53,579
They send only titles and identifiers, never the text, and can be switched off.

45
00:02:54,383 --> 00:02:57,857
Note Atlas also speaks the Model Context Protocol.

46
00:02:57,857 --> 00:03:03,622
A small local server bridges Claude Code and Claude Desktop to the running backend,

47
00:03:03,622 --> 00:03:05,775
over standard input and output.

48
00:03:06,583 --> 00:03:12,416
It exposes nine tools: boards and notes, semantic search, Atlas's own grounded Ask,

49
00:03:12,416 --> 00:03:15,157
the research library, and Research Ask.

50
00:03:15,157 --> 00:03:18,530
Every tool is read-only, except creating a note.

51
00:03:19,317 --> 00:03:20,843
This bridge is optional.

52
00:03:20,843 --> 00:03:22,878
Claude itself runs in the cloud,

53
00:03:22,878 --> 00:03:26,693
so anything it reads through these tools leaves the machine.

54
00:03:26,693 --> 00:03:29,173
The local pipeline never depends on it.

55
00:03:29,983 --> 00:03:34,552
Across every module, accuracy comes from constraint, not model size.

56
00:03:34,552 --> 00:03:39,591
Answers cite their sources, weak retrieval is declined rather than guessed,

57
00:03:39,591 --> 00:03:43,018
and every limitation is backed by a verified quote.

58
00:03:43,817 --> 00:03:47,390
The thesis takeaway: high-accuracy, grounded answers,

59
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:52,649
with no internet connection required, and no document ever leaving the device.
